.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

int server_daemonize(void);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Daemonizes the current process using the standard double\-fork pattern
to ensure the daemon cannot acquire a controlling terminal.
.PP
The sequence is:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]First fork\f[R]: The parent exits immediately via
\f[CR]_exit(0)\f[R].
The child continues.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]setsid()\f[R]: The child becomes a new session leader with no
controlling terminal.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Second fork\f[R]: The session leader exits via \f[CR]_exit(0)\f[R].
The grandchild continues as the daemon \(en it is not a session leader,
so it cannot acquire a controlling terminal by opening a TTY device.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]chdir(\(lq/\(rq)\f[R]: Changes to the root directory to avoid
holding a mount point busy.
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]Close stdio\f[R]: Closes \f[CR]STDIN_FILENO\f[R],
\f[CR]STDOUT_FILENO\f[R], and \f[CR]STDERR_FILENO\f[R].
.IP "6." 3
\f[B]Redirect to /dev/null\f[R]: Opens \f[CR]/dev/null\f[R] and
duplicates it to stdin, stdout, and stderr.
.PP
After this function returns successfully, the process is fully detached
from the controlling terminal and running as a background daemon.
.SH PARAMETERS
None.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] on success in the final daemon process (the
grandchild).
.PP
Returns \f[CR]\-1\f[R] if \f[CR]fork()\f[R] or \f[CR]setsid()\f[R]
fails, with \f[CR]errno\f[R] set.
.PP
\f[B]Note\f[R]: On success, this function does not return in the
original calling process or the intermediate child \(en those processes
exit via \f[CR]_exit(0)\f[R].
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]EAGAIN\f[R] \(en \f[CR]fork()\f[R] failed due to resource limits.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ENOMEM\f[R] \(en \f[CR]fork()\f[R] failed due to insufficient
memory.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]EPERM\f[R] \(en \f[CR]setsid()\f[R] failed (the process is already
a session leader).
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]server_shutdown\f[R](3), \f[CR]server_signal_handler\f[R](3),
\f[CR]fork\f[R](2), \f[CR]setsid\f[R](2), \f[CR]daemon\f[R](3)
.SH NOTES
This function is called early in \f[CR]main()\f[R] when the
\f[CR]\-\-daemon\f[R] flag is set, before signal handlers are installed
and before the socket is created.
This ensures the listening socket is created by the daemon process, not
the parent.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-daemon\f[R] flag is incompatible with \f[CR]\-\-stdio\f[R]
and \f[CR]\-\-fd\f[R] transport modes, which require the original
stdin/stdout or inherited file descriptors.
The server rejects this combination at startup.
.PP
After daemonization, diagnostic output goes nowhere (stderr is
\f[CR]/dev/null\f[R]).
Use \f[CR]\-\-verbose\f[R] with \f[CR]\-\-pidfile\f[R] to debug daemon
issues by examining the PID file and sending signals.
.PP
The PID file (if requested via \f[CR]\-\-pidfile\f[R]) is written after
daemonization and socket creation, so it contains the daemon\(cqs actual
PID.
